From f02636ae05ce8d8031c773fc4f6feb2181daab28 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Stosse <1315-Harvester57@users.noreply.git.frama-c.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 23:50:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [e-acsl] mark const globals read-only through their base
 address

__e_acsl_mark_readonly expects the base address of the block to mark
(in the segment model, eacsl_mark_readonly(ptr) calls
mark_readonly_region(ptr, _block_length(ptr)), where _block_length(ptr)
is the size of the whole enclosing block).

Global_observer.mk_init_function previously handled non-struct types by
passing the global's value (Cil.evar) and structs by emitting one call
per field address:

- for a scalar (`const uint64_t k = 23;`), passing the value makes the
  runtime take 23 as an address and abort with
  "Use of invalid address 0x17 in predicate";
- for a union, the generated cast from the union to void * is rejected
  ("implicit cast: cast from union u to void *");
- for a pointer, the block it points to is marked instead of the pointer;
- for a struct with a bitfield member, Cil.mkAddrOf aborts Frama-C
  ("Taking the address of a bitfield");
- for a multi-field struct, passing the address of any non-first field
  writes read-only shadow bits past the end of the struct (aborting
  under --rt-debug with "Attempt to mark read-only ... bytes past block
  boundaries", and corrupting adjacent global shadow memory without
  --rt-debug);
- for an empty struct, no mark_readonly call is emitted at all.

Remove the per-field struct branch and pass Cil.mkAddrOfVi vi for every
non-array type, keeping Cil.evar for arrays (which decay to their base
address).

Regression test: tests/full-mtracking/const_globals.c.
Updated oracle: tests/concurrency/oracle/gen_sequential_threads.0.c.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
